---
id: RS-007
type: rule
family: rust-reliability
title: plaintext secret field on Deserialize type
version: 1

severity: info
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 7

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.rs]
allowlist: []

detection:
  type: ast
  ast_query: '(field_declaration name: (field_identifier) @field) @target'

related: []
enabled: true
opt_in: true
---

This `Deserialize` field appears to ingest a high-confidence credential into a
plaintext type. Deserialising secrets is legitimate, but prefer redacting or
secret-wrapper types (`SecretString`, `Secret<T>`, `Sensitive<T>`, `Redacted<T>`),
a custom deserialiser that validates and wraps the value, or a separate DTO that
cannot accidentally be logged or re-serialised.

This rule is **opt-in** and intentionally narrow. It recognises obvious plaintext
credential names such as `password`, `client_secret`, `access_token`,
`refresh_token`, `api_key`, `api_token`, and `private_key`, including equivalent
`serde(rename = "...")` names. Manual `Deserialize` implementations and generic
words like `token_count`, `key_path`, and `public_key` are out of scope.
